The `<utility>` basics, without the standard library:

| Name | Does |
|---|---|
| `ca::move(x)` | casts to an rvalue: the callee may take x's resources |
| `ca::forward<T>(x)` | passes x on with the value category it was given |
| `ca::exchange(obj, v)` | stores v in obj, returns the old value |
| `ca::swap(a, b)` | exchanges by move; arrays element by element |
| `ca::addressof(x)` | the address of x, ignoring an overloaded `operator&` |
| `ca::declval<T>()` | a T&& in unevaluated contexts (decltype, requires) |
| `ca::initializer_list<E>` | what a braced list `{1, 2, 3}` binds to |

`ca::exchange` is the idiom of every move constructor in ca: take the other object's members
and leave it empty in one expression.

```c++
#include "ca/integers.hpp"
#include "ca/utility.hpp"

class handle final {
	private:
		int _fd;

	public:
		explicit handle(const int fd) noexcept
		: _fd{fd} {
		}

		handle(handle&& other) noexcept
		: _fd{ca::exchange(other._fd, -1)} {
		}

		[[nodiscard]] auto fd(void) const noexcept -> int {
			return _fd;
		}
};

template <typename... Args>
[[nodiscard]] auto make_handle(Args&&... args) noexcept -> handle {
	return handle{ca::forward<Args>(args)...};
}

[[nodiscard]] constexpr auto total(const ca::initializer_list<int> values) noexcept -> int {
	int sum = 0;
	for (const int v : values)
		sum += v;
	return sum;
}

static_assert(total({1, 2, 3}) == 6);

auto main(void) -> int {
	handle a = make_handle(3);
	handle b{ca::move(a)};
	int x = 1;
	int y = 2;
	ca::swap(x, y);
	return a.fd() == -1 && b.fd() == 3 && x == 2 && y == 1 ? 0 : 1;
}
```

`initializer_list` is the one name ca defines in namespace std: the compiler looks up
`std::initializer_list` by that exact name when it sees a braced list, so it is laid out as
libstdc++ and libc++ lay it out, and used as `ca::initializer_list`.

`type_key.hpp` gives `ca::impl::type_key<T>`, an address unique to T in the whole program:
type identity without RTTI, used where a context must recognize the error type it is given.
